       "content": {
        "id": "v2-proposal-j8-121852-root",
        "type": "question",
        "label": null,
        "prompt": "Differentiate each function using the product rule.\nCombine the terms using a common denominator and factor the numerator completely.\nState the values of $x$ for which the derivative is zero.",
        "children": [
         {
          "id": "v2-proposal-j8-121852-j8-gp-a",
          "type": "part",
          "label": "a",
          "prompt": "$y=-3x\\sqrt{2-x}$",
          "answerSpaceMm": 65,
          "answer": {
           "short": "$y'=\\dfrac{3(3x-4)}{2\\sqrt{2-x}}$; $x=\\dfrac43$.",
           "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}u&=-3x,&u'&=-3\\\\v&=(2-x)^{1/2},&v'&=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2-x}}\\end{aligned}$$\n$$\\begin{aligned}y'&=u'v+uv'\\\\y'&=-3\\sqrt{2-x}+\\frac{3x}{2\\sqrt{2-x}}\\\\y'&=\\frac{-6(2-x)+3x}{2\\sqrt{2-x}}\\\\y'&=\\frac{3(3x-4)}{2\\sqrt{2-x}}\\end{aligned}$$\nFor $y'=0$, $x=\\dfrac43$. This value satisfies $x<2$, so the derivative is zero at $x=\\dfrac43$."
          }
         },
         {
          "id": "v2-proposal-j8-121852-j8-gp-b",
          "type": "part",
          "label": "b",
          "prompt": "$y=x^2\\sqrt{x-3}$",
          "answerSpaceMm": 65,
          "answer": {
           "short": "$y'=\\dfrac{x(5x-12)}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}$. There are no values of $x$ for which the derivative is zero.",
           "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}u&=x^2,&u'&=2x\\\\v&=(x-3)^{1/2},&v'&=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}\\end{aligned}$$\n$$\\begin{aligned}y'&=u'v+uv'\\\\y'&=2x\\sqrt{x-3}+\\frac{x^2}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}\\\\y'&=\\frac{4x(x-3)+x^2}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}\\\\y'&=\\frac{x(5x-12)}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}\\end{aligned}$$\nThe numerator is zero when $x=0$ or $x=\\dfrac{12}{5}$.\nThe derivative requires $x>3$. Neither value is in this domain, so there are no values of $x$ for which the derivative is zero."
          }
         },
         {
          "id": "v2-proposal-j8-121852-j8-gp-c",
          "type": "part",
          "label": "c",
          "prompt": "$y=x\\sqrt{3x+1}$",
          "answerSpaceMm": 65,
          "answer": {
           "short": "$y'=\\dfrac{9x+2}{2\\sqrt{3x+1}}$; $x=-\\dfrac29$.",
           "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}u&=x,&u'&=1\\\\v&=(3x+1)^{1/2},&v'&=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3x+1}}\\end{aligned}$$\n$$\\begin{aligned}y'&=u'v+uv'\\\\y'&=\\sqrt{3x+1}+\\frac{3x}{2\\sqrt{3x+1}}\\\\y'&=\\frac{2(3x+1)+3x}{2\\sqrt{3x+1}}\\\\y'&=\\frac{9x+2}{2\\sqrt{3x+1}}\\end{aligned}$$\nFor $y'=0$, $x=-\\dfrac29$. This value satisfies $x>-\\dfrac13$, so the derivative is zero at $x=-\\dfrac29$."
          }
         }
        ],
